In .NET MAUI, use `Application.Current.RequestedTheme` and the `RequestedThemeChanged` event:

### Detect & subscribe

```csharp
// Current theme
AppTheme current = Application.Current!.RequestedTheme;

// React to system changes in real time
Application.Current.RequestedThemeChanged += (s, e) =>
{
    Debug.WriteLine($"Theme changed to {e.RequestedTheme}");
    // Update UI or re-apply styles here
};
```

### Force a specific theme

```csharp
// Override system setting (Dark, Light, or Unspecified to follow system)
Application.Current!.UserAppTheme = AppTheme.Dark;
```

Setting `UserAppTheme` to `AppTheme.Unspecified` reverts to following the system.

### Using it with `AppThemeBinding` in XAML

```xml
<Label Text="Hello"
       TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light=Black, Dark=White}" />
```

These bindings update automatically when the theme changes — no manual refresh needed.

### Typical pattern (e.g., in `App.xaml.cs`)

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    public App()
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        RequestedThemeChanged += OnThemeChanged;
    }

    void OnThemeChanged(object? sender, AppThemeChangedEventArgs e)
    {
        Log.Info($"System theme is now {e.RequestedTheme}");
        // Any imperative UI updates go here;
        // AppThemeBinding in XAML handles itself automatically.
    }

    public void SetTheme(AppTheme theme) =>
        UserAppTheme = theme; // Dark, Light, or Unspecified
}
```

**Key points:**
- `RequestedTheme` — read-only, reflects the OS setting.
- `UserAppTheme` — read/write, your app-level override.
- `AppThemeBinding` in XAML reacts to both automatically.
- `RequestedThemeChanged` fires when the **system** theme changes (not when you set `UserAppTheme`).
